{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:09:36.488Z","ordinal":49995,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a110dd-a561-7f92-9e34-cf5bd1e8dee9","item":{"type":"Extension","kind":"web.search","id":"exec-7b44f693-3185-49ab-b2fe-b8808f8c014e","query":"https://llmstxt.org/","action":{"type":"openPage","url":"https://llmstxt.org/"},"results":[{"type":"text_result","domain":"llmstxt.org","ref_id":"turn36view0","snippet":"Total lines: 165","thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/REDACTED","title":"The /llms.txt file, v2 – llms-txt","url":"https://llmstxt.org/"},{"type":"text_result","domain":"developers.google.com","ref_id":"turn36view1","snippet":"Total lines: 237","thumbnail_url":"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-1/REDACTED","title":"Robots.txt Introduction and Guide | Google Search Central  |  Documentation  |  Google for Developers","url":"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro"}]},"started_at_ms":1791310174310,"completed_at_ms":1791310176488}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:09:37.357Z","ordinal":49996,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a110dd-a561-7f92-9e34-cf5bd1e8dee9","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6ff35df5-d282-4f89-9054-96849d411d3f","process_id":"16310","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","gh pr view 155 --repo crcorbett/taxkit --json number,url,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,title,body,statusCheckRollup"],"cwd":"file:///Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh pr view 155 --repo crcorbett/taxkit --json number,url,state,isDraft,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,title,body,statusCheckRollup"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"{\"baseRefName\":\"codex/dev-75-shared-rate-limits\",\"body\":\"The replacement Website now renders the 61 accepted documentation pages and lets readers search them through the checked private API connection. Search uses a labelled GET form, works without JavaScript, keeps literal words and Unicode unchanged, and links results back to the documentation. Invalid input and unavailable or damaged replies show fixed recovery guidance.\\n\\nThe pages, navigation, review records and four integration templates retain their saved source hashes. No second search index or collection event is added. The existing native development check now waits for a served page after addresses are printed, with a 15-second limit for 502/503 responses only; the following browser navigation must still return 200. The underlying cause of the earlier hosted 502 has not been established.\\n\\nValidation: `bun run test` passed, including 602 actual lint-rule checks. The freshly built native Website/API suite passed all 11 tests across 7 files, including the retained calculator answers, documentation/search, hostile input, JavaScript-free forms, source reload and cleanup. Independent search design review returned **ship**; its documentation check preserved the existing design. These fresh agents used supplied review instructions because a preset loader was unavailable. The complete `bun run verification` passed, including all 21 Website browser cases and a second fresh run of all 11 native tests. Final documentation, runbook, checkout-path, governance, workflow and whitespace checks also passed after saving the receipt.\\n\\nEvidence: [Website documentation](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-connection.json) and [Website search](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-search.json). Both receipts state their exact source identities, retained sources, local checks and limits.\\n\\nHosted Quality for `b7cbed17` failed at initial Website navigation in [run 37500299332](https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37500299332). The new commit still needs its own hosted result; local checks and the successful parent run do not establish hosted success for this change.\\n\\nDEV-76 remains In Progress. Crawler/discovery files, Markdown negotiation, share images and old-app retirement remain unfinished. Metrics are deferred under Cooper's latest direction; platform collection remains disabled. The Medicare decision remains separate. This draft PR authorises no merge, deployment, publication or provider apply.\\n\\nNo new Changeset is needed for this private Website/test slice: no package export or HTTP/RPC contract changes. The existing native documentation contract Changeset is retained.\\n\",\"headRefName\":\"codex/dev-76-public-content-catalogue\",\"headRefOid\":\"8b32afcae9bd3ac7a93e8d2f63143bc603717961\",\"isDraft\":true,\"number\":155,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37509022712/job/112425009444\",\"name\":\"quality\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-06T18:08:46Z\",\"status\":\"IN_PROGRESS\",\"workflowName\":\"Quality\"}],\"title\":\"[DEV-76] Add checked documentation pages and search\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/pull/155\"}\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"{\"baseRefName\":\"codex/dev-75-shared-rate-limits\",\"body\":\"The replacement Website now renders the 61 accepted documentation pages and lets readers search them through the checked private API connection. Search uses a labelled GET form, works without JavaScript, keeps literal words and Unicode unchanged, and links results back to the documentation. Invalid input and unavailable or damaged replies show fixed recovery guidance.\\n\\nThe pages, navigation, review records and four integration templates retain their saved source hashes. No second search index or collection event is added. The existing native development check now waits for a served page after addresses are printed, with a 15-second limit for 502/503 responses only; the following browser navigation must still return 200. The underlying cause of the earlier hosted 502 has not been established.\\n\\nValidation: `bun run test` passed, including 602 actual lint-rule checks. The freshly built native Website/API suite passed all 11 tests across 7 files, including the retained calculator answers, documentation/search, hostile input, JavaScript-free forms, source reload and cleanup. Independent search design review returned **ship**; its documentation check preserved the existing design. These fresh agents used supplied review instructions because a preset loader was unavailable. The complete `bun run verification` passed, including all 21 Website browser cases and a second fresh run of all 11 native tests. Final documentation, runbook, checkout-path, governance, workflow and whitespace checks also passed after saving the receipt.\\n\\nEvidence: [Website documentation](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-connection.json) and [Website search](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-search.json). Both receipts state their exact source identities, retained sources, local checks and limits.\\n\\nHosted Quality for `b7cbed17` failed at initial Website navigation in [run 37500299332](https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37500299332). The new commit still needs its own hosted result; local checks and the successful parent run do not establish hosted success for this change.\\n\\nDEV-76 remains In Progress. Crawler/discovery files, Markdown negotiation, share images and old-app retirement remain unfinished. Metrics are deferred under Cooper's latest direction; platform collection remains disabled. The Medicare decision remains separate. This draft PR authorises no merge, deployment, publication or provider apply.\\n\\nNo new Changeset is needed for this private Website/test slice: no package export or HTTP/RPC contract changes. The existing native documentation contract Changeset is retained.\\n\",\"headRefName\":\"codex/dev-76-public-content-catalogue\",\"headRefOid\":\"8b32afcae9bd3ac7a93e8d2f63143bc603717961\",\"isDraft\":true,\"number\":155,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37509022712/job/112425009444\",\"name\":\"quality\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-06T18:08:46Z\",\"status\":\"IN_PROGRESS\",\"workflowName\":\"Quality\"}],\"title\":\"[DEV-76] Add checked documentation pages and search\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/pull/155\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":589514416},"formatted_output":"{\"baseRefName\":\"codex/dev-75-shared-rate-limits\",\"body\":\"The replacement Website now renders the 61 accepted documentation pages and lets readers search them through the checked private API connection. Search uses a labelled GET form, works without JavaScript, keeps literal words and Unicode unchanged, and links results back to the documentation. Invalid input and unavailable or damaged replies show fixed recovery guidance.\\n\\nThe pages, navigation, review records and four integration templates retain their saved source hashes. No second search index or collection event is added. The existing native development check now waits for a served page after addresses are printed, with a 15-second limit for 502/503 responses only; the following browser navigation must still return 200. The underlying cause of the earlier hosted 502 has not been established.\\n\\nValidation: `bun run test` passed, including 602 actual lint-rule checks. The freshly built native Website/API suite passed all 11 tests across 7 files, including the retained calculator answers, documentation/search, hostile input, JavaScript-free forms, source reload and cleanup. Independent search design review returned **ship**; its documentation check preserved the existing design. These fresh agents used supplied review instructions because a preset loader was unavailable. The complete `bun run verification` passed, including all 21 Website browser cases and a second fresh run of all 11 native tests. Final documentation, runbook, checkout-path, governance, workflow and whitespace checks also passed after saving the receipt.\\n\\nEvidence: [Website documentation](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-connection.json) and [Website search](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-search.json). Both receipts state their exact source identities, retained sources, local checks and limits.\\n\\nHosted Quality for `b7cbed17` failed at initial Website navigation in [run 37500299332](https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37500299332). The new commit still needs its own hosted result; local checks and the successful parent run do not establish hosted success for this change.\\n\\nDEV-76 remains In Progress. Crawler/discovery files, Markdown negotiation, share images and old-app retirement remain unfinished. Metrics are deferred under Cooper's latest direction; platform collection remains disabled. The Medicare decision remains separate. This draft PR authorises no merge, deployment, publication or provider apply.\\n\\nNo new Changeset is needed for this private Website/test slice: no package export or HTTP/RPC contract changes. The existing native documentation contract Changeset is retained.\\n\",\"headRefName\":\"codex/dev-76-public-content-catalogue\",\"headRefOid\":\"8b32afcae9bd3ac7a93e8d2f63143bc603717961\",\"isDraft\":true,\"number\":155,\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"statusCheckRollup\":[{\"__typename\":\"CheckRun\",\"completedAt\":\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"conclusion\":\"\",\"detailsUrl\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37509022712/job/112425009444\",\"name\":\"quality\",\"startedAt\":\"2026-10-06T18:08:46Z\",\"status\":\"IN_PROGRESS\",\"workflowName\":\"Quality\"}],\"title\":\"[DEV-76] Add checked documentation pages and search\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/pull/155\"}\n"},"started_at_ms":1791310176767,"completed_at_ms":1791310177356}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-06T18:09:37.424Z","ordinal":49997,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a11268-2490-7f30-a027-d1dad560115a","call_id":"call_YoeQ30wqnnCN21Vr7fwPG3Na","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 3.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"The /llms.txt file, v2 – llms-txt (https://llmstxt.org/)\nciteturn36view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://llmstxt.org/\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 165\nL0:   * cite0†The /llms.txt file, v2 L1: \nL2:   * cite1†Changes L3: \nL4:   *     * cite2†Python module & CLI L5: \nL6:     * cite3†Python source L7: \nL8:     * cite4†JavaScript L9: \nL10:   *     * cite5†`ed`, the standard text editor L11: \nL12:   *     * cite6†llms.txt in Different Domains L13: \nL14:     * cite7†How to help LLMs understand your nbdev project L15: \nL16: ## On this page\nL17: \nL18:   * cite8†Background L19:   * cite9†Proposal L20:   * cite10†Format L21:   * cite11†Existing standards L22:   * cite12†Example L23:   * cite13†Directories L24:   * cite14†Integrations L25:   * cite15†Next steps L26: \nL27:   * cite16†Report an issue†github.com L28: ## Other Formats\nL29: \nL30:   * cite17†Markdown L31: \nL32: # The /llms.txt file, v2\nL33: \nL34: A proposal to standardise on using an `/llms.txt` file to provide information to help agents use a website.\nL35: \nL36: Author\nL37: \nL38: Jeremy Howard\nL39: \nL40: Published\nL41: \nL42: September 3, 2024\nL43: \nL44: Modified\nL45: \nL46: August 10, 2026\nL47: ## Background\nL48: \nL49: Agents now use websites constantly: a coding agent fetches a library’s documentation to get an API call right, and a chat assistant with search reads pages to answer questions about a product. When this proposal was first written in 2024, this was largely a prediction. Today it is routine.\nL50: But web pages are built for people. An HTML page wraps its information in navigation, ads, and JavaScript, and converting it back into clean text is difficult and imprecise. Context windows, while larger than they were, are still too small for most websites in their entirety, and every wasted token costs time and money. Agents are best served by concise, expert-level information gathered in a single, accessible location.\nL51: This is v2 of the proposal, updated based on what I learned from two years of adoption: thousands of sites publish an llms.txt file, cite18†documentation platforms generate one automatically†www.mintlify.com , and Chrome’s Lighthouse cite19†audits sites for one†developer.chrome.com as part of its agentic browsing checks. The AI labs themselves publish llms.txt files for their own developer docs: cite20†OpenAI†developers.openai.com , cite21†Anthropic†docs.anthropic.com , and cite22†Gemini†ai.google.dev .\nL52: The cite1†Changes page describes what changed since v1, and why.\nL53: ## Proposal\nL54: \nL55: llms.txt logo\nL56: \nL57: We propose adding a `/llms.txt` markdown file to websites to provide LLM-friendly content. The file can be placed at the site root, or at any path within it, covering the pages under that path. This file offers brief background information, guidance, and links to detailed markdown files.\nL58: \nL59: llms.txt markdown is human and LLM readable, but is also in a precise format allowing fixed processing methods (i.e. classical programming techniques such as parsers and regex).\nL60: We furthermore propose that pages with information that agents might need provide a clean markdown version of those pages at the same URL as the original page, either with `.md` appended (`page.html.md`) or with the extension replaced by `.md` (`page.md`). (URLs without file names should append `index.html.md` or `index.md` instead.)\nL61: To help clients find these files, we recommend using standard link relations: `rel=\"alternate\" type=\"text/markdown\"` points to the markdown version of a page, and `rel=\"describedby\"` points to the llms.txt file that covers it. (An llms.txt file describes all pages under its path, so `/docs/llms.txt` covers everything in `/docs/`.) These links can be provided as HTML `<link>` elements, or as an HTTP `Link:` response header.\nL62: The header form also works for non-HTML resources, such as the markdown files themselves, and can be added in web server or CDN configuration without modifying any pages. For example:\nL63: \nL64:     `Link: </docs/page.html.md>; rel=\"alternate\"; type=\"text/markdown\", </docs/llms.txt>; rel=\"describedby\"`\nL65: The cite23†FastHTML project†fastht.ml follows these two proposals for its documentation. For instance, here is the cite24†FastHTML docs llms.txt†www.fastht.ml , placed at `/docs/` to cover just the documentation pages. And here is an example of a cite25†regular HTML docs page†www.fastht.ml , along with exact same URL but with cite26†a .md extension†www.fastht.ml .\nL66: Agents are expected to view or search `llms.txt` to find the information they need, then follow the relevant links. The links in an llms.txt file should therefore point to LLM-friendly content, such as the markdown versions of pages described above. The file itself stays small enough to fit in context. The detail lives behind the links, and is fetched only when needed.\nL67: llms.txt files are used most heavily for software documentation, where coding agents follow them to find API references and tutorials. The same structure works anywhere agents need a guided path into a site’s content: a business outlining its structure and policies, a personal site answering questions about someone’s CV, or a school providing access to course information.\nL68: Note that all cite27†nbdev†nbdev.fast.ai projects now create .md versions of all pages by default. All Answer.AI and fast.ai software projects using nbdev have had their docs regenerated with this feature. For an example, see the cite28†markdown version†fastcore.fast.ai of cite29†fastcore’s docments module†fastcore.fast.ai .\nL69: ## Format\nL70: \nL71: At the moment the most widely and easily understood format for language models is Markdown. Simply showing where key Markdown files can be found is a great first step. Providing some basic structure helps a language model to find where the information it needs can come from.\nL72: The `llms.txt` file is unusual in that it uses Markdown to structure the information rather than a classic structured format such as XML. The reason for this is that we expect many of these files to be read by language models and agents. Having said that, the information in llms.txt follows a specific format and can be read using standard programmatic-based tools.\nL73: The llms.txt file spec is for files named `llms.txt`, at the root path `/llms.txt` of a website or at any subpath (e.g. `/docs/llms.txt`). A file covers the URLs under its path, and where more than one file applies, agents should use the most specific one. A file following the spec contains the following sections as markdown, in the specific order:\nL74:   * An optional byte-order mark (BOM)\nL75:   * An H1 with the name of the project or site. This is the only required section\nL76:   * A blockquote with a short summary of the project, containing key information necessary for understanding the rest of the file\nL77:   * Zero or more markdown sections (e.g. paragraphs, lists, etc) of any type except headings, containing more detailed information about the project and how to interpret the provided files\nL78:   * Zero or more markdown sections delimited by H2 headers, containing “file lists” of URLs where further detail is available\nL79:     * Each “file list” is a markdown list, containing a required markdown hyperlink `[name](url)`, then optionally a `:` and notes about the file.\nL80: Here is a mock example:\nL81: \nL82:     `# Title\nL83: \nL84:     > Optional description goes here\nL85: \nL86:     Optional details go here\nL87: \nL88:     ## Section name\nL89: \nL90:     - [Link title](https://link_url): Optional link details\nL91: \nL92:     ## Optional\nL93: \nL94:     - [Link title](https://link_url)`\nL95: \nL96: The “Optional” section is used, by convention, for secondary information: links an agent can skip when a shorter context is needed.\nL97: ## Existing standards\nL98: \nL99: llms.txt is designed to coexist with current web standards. While sitemaps list all pages for search engines, `llms.txt` offers a curated overview for LLMs. It can complement robots.txt by providing context for allowed content. The file can also reference structured data markup used on the site, helping LLMs understand how to interpret this information in context.\nL100: The approach of using a standard filename follows `/robots.txt` and `/sitemap.xml` at the site root. And just as `index.html` gives any path a conventional location for its human-readable entry point, `llms.txt` gives any path a conventional location for its LLM-readable overview. robots.txt and `llms.txt` have different purposes. robots.txt lets automated tools know what access to a site is considered acceptable, such as for search indexing bots.\nL101: llms.txt information is instead used on demand, when an agent needs information about a topic while assisting a user. Our expectation was that llms.txt would mainly be useful for inference rather than training, and that is how it has been used, though training runs could take advantage of the information too.\nL102: An alternative would be the Well-Known URIs standard (RFC 8615), which reserves the `/.well-known/` prefix for metadata files like this one. But well-known URIs exist only at the origin root, and many authors control only a path on a shared host: a GitHub Pages project site, for example, can publish files in its own directory but can never add one to the host’s `/.well-known/`. Like `index.html`, an `llms.txt` describes the path where it sits, something a single root location cannot express.\nL103: And anyone who can publish content at a path can provide one.\nL104: sitemap.xml is a list of all the indexable human-readable information available on a site. This isn’t a substitute for `llms.txt` since it:\nL105: \nL106:   * Often won’t have the LLM-readable versions of pages listed\nL107:   * Doesn’t include URLs to external sites, even though they might be helpful to understand the information\nL108:   * Will generally cover documents that in aggregate will be too large to fit in an LLM context window, and will include a lot of information that isn’t necessary to understand the site.\nL109: ## Example\nL110: Here’s an example of `llms.txt`, in this case a cut down version of the file used for the FastHTML project (see also the cite24†full version†www.fastht.ml ):\nL111: \nL112:     `# FastHTML\nL113: \nL114:     > FastHTML is a python library which brings together Starlette, Uvicorn, HTMX, and fastcore's `FT` \"FastTags\" into a library for creating server-rendered hypermedia applications.\nL115: \nL116:     Important notes:\nL117:     - Although parts of its API are inspired by FastAPI, it is *not* compatible with FastAPI syntax and is not targeted at creating API services\nL118:     - FastHTML is compatible with JS-native web components and any vanilla JS library, but not with React, Vue, or Svelte.\nL119: \nL120:     ## Docs\nL121: \nL122:     - [FastHTML quick start](https://fastht.ml/docs/tutorials/quickstart_for_web_devs.html.md): A brief overview of many FastHTML features\nL123:     - [HTMX reference](https://github.com/bigskysoftware/htmx/blob/master/www/content/reference.md): Brief description of all HTMX attributes, CSS classes, headers, events, extensions, js lib methods, and config options\nL124: \nL125:     ## Examples\nL126: \nL127:     - [Todo list application](https://github.com/AnswerDotAI/fasthtml/blob/main/examples/adv_app.py): Detailed walk-thru of a complete CRUD app in FastHTML showing idiomatic use of FastHTML and HTMX patterns.\nL128: \nL129:     ## Optional\nL130:     - [Starlette full documentation](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/jph00/809e4a4808d4510be0e3dc9565e9cbd3/raw/9b717589ca44cedc8aaf00b2b8cacef922964c0f/starlette-sml.md): A subset of the Starlette documentation useful for FastHTML development. `\nL131: To create effective `llms.txt` files, consider these guidelines:\nL132: \nL133:   * Use concise, clear language.\nL134:   * When linking to resources, include brief, informative descriptions.\nL135:   * Avoid ambiguous terms or unexplained jargon.\nL136:   * Test your file by asking an agent questions about your content, giving it only your llms.txt as a starting point.\nL137: ## Directories\nL138: \nL139: Here are a few directories that list the `llms.txt` files available on the web:\nL140: \nL141:   * cite30†llmstxt.site†llmstxt.site L142:   * cite31†directory.llmstxt.cloud†directory.llmstxt.cloud L143:   * cite32†llmstxthub.com†llmstxthub.com L144: ## Integrations\nL145: \nL146: Many documentation platforms and CMSs can generate an llms.txt file automatically:\nL147:   * cite18†Mintlify†www.mintlify.com - Docs platform that generates llms.txt and markdown page versions for every site it hosts\nL148:   * cite33†GitBook†www.gitbook.com - Serves an llms.txt file for published docs sites\nL149:   * cite34†Yoast SEO†yoast.com - WordPress plugin that generates and maintains an llms.txt file\nL150:   * cite35†AIOSEO†aioseo.com - WordPress plugin with an llms.txt generator\nL151:   * cite36†Wix†support.wix.com - Generates an llms.txt file for every Wix site\nL152: And various libraries and plugins are available to integrate the llms.txt specification into your workflow:\nL153:   * cite4†JavaScript Implementation - Sample JavaScript implementation\nL154:   * cite37†`vitepress-plugin-llms`†github.com - VitePress plugin that automatically generates LLM-friendly documentation for the website following the llms.txt specification\nL155:   * cite38†`docusaurus-plugin-llms`†github.com - Docusaurus plugin for generating LLM-friendly documentation following the llmtxt.org standard\nL156:   * cite39†Drupal LLM Support†www.drupal.org - A Drupal Recipe providing full support for the llms.txt proposal on any Drupal 10.3+ site\nL157:   * cite40†`llms-txt-php`†github.com - A library for writing and reading llms.txt Markdown files\nL158:   * cite41†`VS Code PagePilot Extension`†dmux.github.io - PagePilot is a VS Code Chat participant that automatically loads external context (documentation, APIs, README files) to provide enhanced responses.\nL159:   * cite42†`server-llm-txt`†github.com - MCP server that lets agents fetch and search llms.txt files\nL160: ## Next steps\nL161: \nL162: The `llms.txt` specification is open for community input. A cite43†GitHub repository†github.com hosts cite44†this informal overview†github.com , allowing for version control and public discussion. A cite45†community discord channel†discord.gg is available for sharing implementation experiences and discussing best practices.\nL163: \nL164:   * cite16†Report an issue†github.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRobots.txt Introduction and Guide | Google Search Central  |  Documentation  |  Google for Developers (https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro)\nciteturn36view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/robots/intro\",\"lineno\":123}); Total lines: 237\nL0: [Button: ]\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Documentation cite1†Support cite2†Blog cite3†What's new cite4†Events cite5†Case studies L3: \nL4: [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: `/`\nL7: \nL8:   * English\nL9:   * Deutsch\nL10:   * Español\nL11:   * Español – América Latina\nL12:   * Français\nL13:   * Indonesia\nL14:   * Italiano\nL15:   * Polski\nL16:   * Português – Brasil\nL17:   * Tiếng Việt\nL18:   * Türkçe\nL19:   * Русский\nL20:   * العربيّة\nL21:   * हिंदी\nL22:   * ภาษาไทย\nL23:   * 中文 – 简体\nL24:   * 中文 – 繁體\nL25:   * 日本語\nL26:   * 한국어\nL27: Sign in\nL28: \nL29: cite6†Search Console†goo.gle L30: \nL31: [Input: Filter]\nL32: \nL33: [Button: ]\nL34: \nL35:   * Search Essentials\nL36: \nL37:   * SEO fundamentals\nL38:     * Generative AI fundamentals\nL39: \nL40:   * Crawling and indexing\nL41: \nL42:     * Sitemaps\nL43: \nL44:       * Sitemap extensions\nL45: \nL46:     * Crawler management\nL47: \nL48:       * Google crawlers\nL49: \nL50:         * cite7†List of Google crawlers L51:         * cite8†Reduce the Google crawl rate L52:         * cite9†Verifying Googlebot and other Google crawlers L53: \nL54:     * robots.txt\nL55: \nL56:       * cite10†How Google interprets the robots.txt specification L57: \nL58:     * Canonicalization\nL59: \nL60:       * cite11†How to specify a canonical URL with rel=\"canonical\" and other methods L61: \nL62:     * AMP\nL63:     * JavaScript\nL64: \nL65:       * cite12†Dynamic rendering as a workaround L66: \nL67:     * Page and content metadata\nL68: \nL69:       * Meta tags\nL70: \nL71:     * Removals\nL72: \nL73:     * Site moves and changes\nL74: \nL75:       * Site moves\nL76: \nL77:   * Ranking and search appearance\nL78: \nL79:     * Local features\nL80: \nL81:       * cite13†Opting out of local search results†support.google.com L82: \nL83:     * Page experience\nL84: \nL85:     * Ranking systems\nL86: \nL87:     * Ranking updates\nL88: \nL89:       * cite14†List of ranking updates†status.search.google.com L90: \nL91:     * Regional differences in Search experience\nL92:     * Structured data\nL93: \nL94:       * Feature guides\nL95: \nL96:         * cite15†Book actions L97:         * Shopping\nL98: \nL99:         * cite16†Speakable L100:         * cite17†Vacation rental L101: \nL102:     * Translated features\nL103: \nL104:       * cite18†Ad networks and translation-related Google Search features L105: \nL106:     * Web Stories\nL107: \nL108:     * Early Adopters Program\nL109: \nL110:       * cite19†Package tracking L111: \nL112:   * Monitoring and debugging\nL113: \nL114:     * Monitor with Search Console\nL115: \nL116:     * Debug with search operators\nL117: \nL118:     * Preventing and monitoring abuse\nL119: \nL120:   * Site-specific guides\nL121:     * Ecommerce\nL122: \nL123:     * International and multilingual\nL124: \nL125:     * Explicit content\nL126: \nL127: [Button: Send feedback ]\nL128: # Introduction to robots.txt\nL129: \nL130: A robots.txt file tells search engine crawlers which URLs the crawler can access on your site. This is used mainly to avoid overloading your site with requests; it is not a mechanism for keeping a web page out of Google. To keep a web page out of Google, cite20†block indexing with `noindex` or password-protect the page.\nL131: If you use a CMS, such as Wix or Blogger, you might not need to (or be able to) edit your robots.txt file directly. Instead, your CMS might expose a search settings page or some other mechanism to tell search engines whether or not to crawl your page.\nL132: \nL133: If you want to hide or unhide one of your pages from search engines, search for instructions about modifying your page visibility in search engines on your CMS (for example, search for \"wix hide page from search engines\").\nL134: ## What is a robots.txt file used for?\nL135: \nL136: A robots.txt file is used primarily to manage crawler traffic to your site, and usually to keep a file off Google, depending on the file type:\nL137: robots.txt effect on different file types\nL138: ---\nL139: Web page  | You can use a robots.txt file for web pages (HTML, PDF, or other cite21†non-media formats that Google can read ), to manage crawling traffic if you think your server will be overwhelmed by requests from Google's crawler, or to avoid crawling unimportant or similar pages on your site. Warning: Don't use a robots.txt file as a means to hide your web pages (including PDFs and other text-based formats supported by Google) from Google Search results.\nL140: If other pages point to your page with descriptive text, Google could still index the URL without visiting the page. If you want to block your page from search results, use another method such as password protection or cite20†`noindex` . If your web page is blocked with a robots.txt file, its URL can still appear in search results, but the search result cite22†won't have a description†support.google.com .\nL141: Image files, video files, PDFs, and other non-HTML files embedded in the blocked page will be excluded from crawling, too, unless they're referenced by other pages that are allowed for crawling. If you see this search result for your page and want to fix it, remove the robots.txt entry blocking the page. If you want to hide the page completely from Search, use cite23†another method .\nL142: Media file  | Use a robots.txt file to manage crawl traffic, and also to prevent image, video, and audio files from appearing in Google Search results. This won't prevent other pages or users from linking to your image, video, or audio file.\nL143:   * cite24†Read more about preventing images from appearing on Google. L144:   * cite25†Read more about how to remove or restrict your video files from appearing on Google. L145: Resource file  | You can use a robots.txt file to block resource files such as unimportant image, script, or style files, if you think that pages loaded without these resources won't be significantly affected by the loss. However, if the absence of these resources make the page harder for Google's crawler to understand the page, don't block them, or else Google won't do a good job of analyzing pages that depend on those resources.\nL146: ## Understand the limitations of a robots.txt file\nL147: \nL148: Before you create or edit a robots.txt file, you should know the limits of this URL blocking method. Depending on your goals and situation, you might want to consider other mechanisms to ensure your URLs are not findable on the web.\nL149:   * robots.txt rules may not be supported by all search engines.\nL150: The instructions in robots.txt files cannot enforce crawler behavior to your site; it's up to the crawler to obey them. While Googlebot and other respectable web crawlers obey the instructions in a robots.txt file, other crawlers might not. Therefore, if you want to keep information secure from web crawlers, it's better to use other blocking methods, such as cite26†password-protecting private files on your server .\nL151:   * Different crawlers interpret syntax differently.\nL152: Although respectable web crawlers follow the rules in a robots.txt file, each crawler might interpret the rules differently. You should know the cite27†proper syntax for addressing different web crawlers as some might not understand certain instructions.\nL153:   * A page that's disallowed in robots.txt can still be indexed if linked to from other sites.\nL154: While Google won't crawl or index the content blocked by a robots.txt file, we might still find and index a disallowed URL if it is linked from other places on the web. As a result, the URL address and, potentially, other publicly available information such as anchor text in links to the page can still appear in Google Search results.\nL155: To properly prevent your URL from appearing in Google Search results, cite26†password-protect the files on your server , cite20†use the `noindex` `meta` tag or response header , or remove the page entirely.\nL156: Caution: Combining multiple crawling and indexing rules might cause some rules to counteract other rules. Learn how to cite28†combine crawling with indexing and serving rules .\nL157: ## Create or update a robots.txt file\nL158: \nL159: If you decided that you need one, learn how to cite29†create a robots.txt file . Or if you already have one, learn how to cite30†update it .\nL160: \nL161: Want to learn more? Check out the following resources:\nL162: \nL163:   * cite31†How to write and submit a robots.txt file L164:   * cite32†Update your robots.txt file L165:   * cite10†How Google interprets the robots.txt specification L166: \nL167: [Button: Send feedback ]\nL168: Except as otherwise noted, the content of this page is licensed under the cite33†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License†creativecommons.org , and code samples are licensed under the cite34†Apache 2.0 License†www.apache.org . For details, see the cite35†Google Developers Site Policies . Java is a registered trademark of Oracle and/or its affiliates.\nL169: \nL170: Last updated 2025-12-10 UTC.\nL171: \nL172:   * cite36†LinkedIn†www.linkedin.com L173: \nL174: Join us on LinkedIn\nL175: \nL176:   * cite37†YouTube†www.youtube.com L177: \nL178: Watch our videos\nL179:   * cite38†Blog†feeds.feedburner.com L180: \nL181: Subscribe to our RSS feed\nL182: \nL183:   * cite39†Podcast†pod.link L184: \nL185: Listen to Search Off the Record\nL186: \nL187:   * cite40†X (Twitter)†twitter.com L188: \nL189: Join us on X (Twitter)\nL190: \nL191:   * ### Get support\nL192: \nL193:     * cite41†Go to the help forum†support.google.com L194:     * cite42†Submit a question for office hours L195:     * cite43†Report spam, phishing, or malware L196:     * cite1†More support resources L197:   * ### Resources\nL198:     * cite44†Do you need an SEO? L199:     * cite45†SEO Starter Guide L200:     * cite46†Status of Search systems†status.search.google.com L201:     * cite47†Search Console documentation†support.google.com L202:     * cite5†Case Studies L203:   * ### Tools\nL204: \nL205:     * cite48†Search Console†search.google.com L206:     * cite49†Rich Results Test†search.google.com L207:     * cite50†PageSpeed Insights†pagespeed.web.dev L208:     * cite51†AMP Test†search.google.com L209:   * cite52†Android†developer.android.com L210:   * cite53†Chrome†developer.chrome.com L211:   * cite54†Firebase†firebase.google.com L212:   * cite55†Google Cloud Platform†cloud.google.com L213:   * cite56†Google AI†ai.google.dev L214:   * cite57†All products L215: \nL216:   * cite58†Terms L217:   * cite59†Privacy†policies.google.com L218:   * English\nL219:   * Deutsch\nL220:   * Español\nL221:   * Español – América Latina\nL222:   * Français\nL223:   * Indonesia\nL224:   * Italiano\nL225:   * Polski\nL226:   * Português – Brasil\nL227:   * Tiếng Việt\nL228:   * Türkçe\nL229:   * Русский\nL230:   * العربيّة\nL231:   * हिंदी\nL232:   * ภาษาไทย\nL233:   * 中文 – 简体\nL234:   * 中文 – 繁體\nL235:   * 日本語\nL236:   * 한국어\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"6bf0a1\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000006542,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":12,\"output\":\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/pull/155\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"a75733\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.589552916,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":830,\"output\":\"{\\\"baseRefName\\\":\\\"codex/dev-75-shared-rate-limits\\\",\\\"body\\\":\\\"The replacement Website now renders the 61 accepted documentation pages and lets readers search them through the checked private API connection. Search uses a labelled GET form, works without JavaScript, keeps literal words and Unicode unchanged, and links results back to the documentation. Invalid input and unavailable or damaged replies show fixed recovery guidance.\\\\n\\\\nThe pages, navigation, review records and four integration templates retain their saved source hashes. No second search index or collection event is added. The existing native development check now waits for a served page after addresses are printed, with a 15-second limit for 502/503 responses only; the following browser navigation must still return 200. The underlying cause of the earlier hosted 502 has not been established.\\\\n\\\\nValidation: `bun run test` passed, including 602 actual lint-rule checks. The freshly built native Website/API suite passed all 11 tests across 7 files, including the retained calculator answers, documentation/search, hostile input, JavaScript-free forms, source reload and cleanup. Independent search design review returned **ship**; its documentation check preserved the existing design. These fresh agents used supplied review instructions because a preset loader was unavailable. The complete `bun run verification` passed, including all 21 Website browser cases and a second fresh run of all 11 native tests. Final documentation, runbook, checkout-path, governance, workflow and whitespace checks also passed after saving the receipt.\\\\n\\\\nEvidence: [Website documentation](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-connection.json) and [Website search](docs/documentation-audit/clean-slate-foundation/2026-10-07-website-docs-search.json). Both receipts state their exact source identities, retained sources, local checks and limits.\\\\n\\\\nHosted Quality for `b7cbed17` failed at initial Website navigation in [run 37500299332](https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37500299332). The new commit still needs its own hosted result; local checks and the successful parent run do not establish hosted success for this change.\\\\n\\\\nDEV-76 remains In Progress. Crawler/discovery files, Markdown negotiation, share images and old-app retirement remain unfinished. Metrics are deferred under Cooper's latest direction; platform collection remains disabled. The Medicare decision remains separate. This draft PR authorises no merge, deployment, publication or provider apply.\\\\n\\\\nNo new Changeset is needed for this private Website/test slice: no package export or HTTP/RPC contract changes. The existing native documentation contract Changeset is retained.\\\\n\\\",\\\"headRefName\\\":\\\"codex/dev-76-public-content-catalogue\\\",\\\"headRefOid\\\":\\\"8b32afcae9bd3ac7a93e8d2f63143bc603717961\\\",\\\"isDraft\\\":true,\\\"number\\\":155,\\\"state\\\":\\\"OPEN\\\",\\\"statusCheckRollup\\\":[{\\\"__typename\\\":\\\"CheckRun\\\",\\\"completedAt\\\":\\\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\\\",\\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"detailsUrl\\\":\\\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/actions/runs/37509022712/job/112425009444\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"quality\\\",\\\"startedAt\\\":\\\"2026-10-06T18:08:46Z\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"IN_PROGRESS\\\",\\\"workflowName\\\":\\\"Quality\\\"}],\\\"title\\\":\\\"[DEV-76] Add checked documentation pages and search\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://github.com/crcorbett/taxkit/pull/155\\\"}\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a110dd-a561-7f92-9e34-cf5bd1e8dee9","create_time":1791310177.424609}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
